#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# exp_to_trig

#% text
# `exp_to_trig(e)` writes every exponential in `e` with circular and hyperbolic functions: `exp(a + im*x)` is `(cosh(a) + sinh(a))*(cos(x) + im*sin(x))` (Euler's formula and `exp(a) = cosh(a) + sinh(a)`). `exp_to_trig(e, hyperbolic: false)` rewrites only the exponentials with an imaginary part.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The guaranteed form: no `exp` with a symbolic argument is left, and the result is expanded (products of sums multiplied out), through lists, rules, equations, inequalities and logical connectives.

#% item
# A power `q^u` of a positive rational number `q` other than 1 (and an exponent that is not a real number) is `exp(u*log(q))` first: `3^x` is `cosh(x*log(3)) + sinh(x*log(3))`.

#% item
# A power `(-1)^u` is `exp(im*pi*u)` (the principal value); for a rational `u` the angle `u*pi` is written in `[0, pi/4]` when its cosine has no closed value: `(-1)^(5/7)` is `-sin((3/14)*pi) + im*cos((3/14)*pi)`.

#% item
# A number stays a number: `exp(2)` is not rewritten, and in `exp(2 + im*x)` the factor `exp(2)` stays.

#% item
# Differences of logarithms that are inverse functions are written as such.

#% item
# With `hyperbolic: false`, exponentials of real arguments and the powers stay as they are.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(im*x))
#% output
#= cos(x) + im*sin(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(-im*x))
#% output
#= cos(x) - im*sin(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig((exp(im*x) + exp(-im*x))/2)
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(x) + exp(-x))
#% output
#= 2*cosh(x)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Powers of positive rational numbers and of -1:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(3^x - 3^(-x))
#% output
#= 2*sinh(x*log(3))

#% cell
exp_to_trig(2^(im*x))
#% output
#= cos(x*log(2)) + im*sin(x*log(2))

#% cell
exp_to_trig((-1)^(1/11))
#% output
#= cos((1/11)*pi) + im*sin((1/11)*pi)

#% cell
exp_to_trig((-1)^(5/7))
#% output
#= -sin((3/14)*pi) + im*cos((3/14)*pi)

#% text
# A difference of logarithms that is an inverse function (the midpoint of the two arguments a positive number):

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(1 + im*x) - log(1 - im*x))
#% output
#= 2im*atan(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(2 + x) - log(2 - x))
#% output
#= 2*atanh((1/2)*x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(z + sqrt(z^2 + 1)))
#% output
#= asinh(z)

#% text
# A complex exponent with a real part:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp((1 + im)*t))
#% output
#= cos(t)*cosh(t) + cos(t)*sinh(t) + im*cosh(t)*sin(t) + im*sin(t)*sinh(t)

#% text
# Inside rules and equations:

#% cell
exp_to_trig([y(t) -> a*exp(t) + b*exp(-t), exp(im*x) == -1])
#% output
#= [y(t) -> a*cosh(t) + a*sinh(t) + b*cosh(t) - b*sinh(t), cos(x) + im*sin(x) == -1]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# `hyperbolic: false` keeps the exponentials of real arguments:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp((1 + im)*t), hyperbolic: false)
#% output
#= cos(t)*exp(t) + im*exp(t)*sin(t)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `trig_to_exp` goes the other way:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(trig_to_exp(cos(x)))
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `trig_to_exp`, `exp`, `cos`, `sin`, `cosh`, `sinh`
